EC2 Burstable vs Non-Burstable Instances
First, remember:
- Burstable instance: Can temporarily use more CPU than its baseline performance when additional CPU capacity is needed.
- Non-burstable instance: Provides consistent CPU capacity according to its configured vCPU resources, rather than relying on CPU credits.


1. What exactly is CPU bursting?
Imagine you have a t3.medium EC2 instance.

It has:
- 2 vCPUs
- 20% baseline CPU utilization per vCPU
- CPU credits that it earns when running below its baseline.
Suppose your application normally uses 10% CPU, but suddenly traffic increases and CPU usage needs to reach 80%.

The T-family instance can use accumulated CPU credits to burst above its baseline.
